---
// Rung 5 of the SIP ladder: "Does it matter WHEN the bad years come?"
//
// The mirror of the planner's rung 4, and the mirror is the point. A retiree is
// ruined by a crash EARLY - forced to sell units cheap that can never be bought
// back. A saver is barely scratched by an early crash, because the pot is small
// and every instalment for the next twenty years buys in at the lower price.
// What costs a saver is a crash LATE, when the pot is at its largest and there
// are no contributions left to buy the recovery with.
//
// Two decisions worth recording, both taken before this file was written and
// both recorded in RungSipSequence.checks.md:
//
// 1. NO SLIDER OVER THE CRASH YEAR. The obvious control is "when does it
//    happen", and it would hide seventeen of eighteen answers behind a thumb,
//    leaving the reader to assemble the shape from memory - dd-006 exactly. The
//    shape IS the lesson, so the whole profile is drawn at once and the control
//    goes to the thing that genuinely has alternatives: how long the run lasts.
//
// 2. THE SETBACK METRIC, after the obvious one failed. The first attempt was
//    the delay to the goal, and it collapsed to "never" on the page's own
//    shipped inputs, which do not reach the goal even without a crash. A
//    headline that goes blank on the default is not a headline.
---

<section id="rungSipSequence" class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-4" aria-labelledby="rungSipSeqQ">
  <div class="card-premium rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-6">

    <div>
      <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.11em] text-navy-400 dark:text-navy-500">
        Next question
      </p>
      <h2 id="rungSipSeqQ" class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100 mt-1">
        Does it matter <em>when</em> the bad years come?
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400 mt-2">
        The same bad years, moved to a different point in the same plan.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- dd-009: name the thing before using it, and say how often it happens.
         The planner's rung 4 failed review for showing a -30% year without ever
         saying what one was. -->
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-navy-50/70 dark:bg-navy-900/40 border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800 px-4 py-3 space-y-1.5">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy-800 dark:text-navy-200">
        <span class="font-semibold">A bad year here means the market falls 30%.</span>
        India has had four of those since 1990 — 1996, 2000, 2008 and 2020 — so
        roughly one decade in three contains one.
      </p>
      <p id="sipSeqFrame" class="text-xs text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400 leading-relaxed">
        Everything on this step assumes a perfectly smooth market apart from the
        bad years themselves, so that the only thing changing is <em>when</em>
        they land. That makes the figures here different from the answer at the
        top of the page, which allows for ordinary ups and downs in every year.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- The one control: how long the bad run lasts. -->
    <div class="space-y-2">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy-700 dark:text-navy-300">How long does the bad run last?</p>
      <div id="sipSeqDurations" role="group" aria-label="Length of the bad run"
           class="inline-flex rounded-lg border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-700 overflow-hidden">
      </div>
      <p id="sipSeqAvg" class="text-xs text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400"></p>
    </div>

    <!-- The whole profile, at once. Bar length encodes one thing only. -->
    <div class="space-y-2">
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100">
          Years of saving lost
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400">by the year the fall begins</p>
      </div>
      <div id="sipSeqBars" class="space-y-1"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Fixed slots. The eye returns to the same two rows. -->
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-700 divide-y divide-navy-200 dark:divide-navy-700 overflow-hidden">
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 px-4 py-3">
        <span class="text-sm text-navy-700 dark:text-navy-300">
          If it comes at the very start
          <span id="sipSeqBestEnd" class="block text-xs text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400"></span>
        </span>
        <span id="sipSeqBest" class="text-lg font-bold tabular-nums text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400">—</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 px-4 py-3.5 bg-navy-50/60 dark:bg-navy-900/40">
        <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100">
          If it comes at the very end
          <span id="sipSeqWorstEnd" class="block text-xs font-normal text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400"></span>
        </span>
        <span id="sipSeqWorst" class="text-lg font-bold tabular-nums text-rose-600 dark:text-rose-400">—</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- The boundary: both ends of the range in one sentence, so no position of
         the control invalidates it (dd-008). -->
    <p id="sipSeqBoundary"
       class="rounded-lg bg-gold-500/10 border border-gold-500/30 px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-navy-800 dark:text-navy-200">
      Working out what the timing costs…
    </p>

    <!-- The principle. True at every position of the control. -->
    <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-navy-800 dark:text-navy-200">
      This is the opposite of what a retiree faces, and it is worth holding on
      to. Someone drawing an income is ruined by a fall at the <em>start</em> —
      they must sell cheap, and those units never come back. You are doing the
      reverse. For as long as you are still buying, a cheap market is a discount
      on everything you have not bought yet, and the money you put in during a
      bad year does the heaviest lifting in the whole plan.
    </p>
    <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-navy-800 dark:text-navy-200">
      What you cannot absorb is a fall near the end. By then the pot is at its
      largest, so the same percentage takes the most money — and there are no
      instalments left to buy the recovery with. The bad year that frightens
      people is the one at the beginning. The one that costs them is the one at
      the finish.
    </p>

    <div class="pt-4 border-t border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-800">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400">
        Which raises the obvious question:
        <span class="font-medium text-navy-800 dark:text-navy-200">can you buy protection from that?</span>
      </p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<script>
  const $ = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id);

  function money(n: number): string {
    const v = Math.round(n);
    if (v >= 10000000) return `₹${(v / 10000000).toFixed(2)} cr`;
    if (v >= 100000) return `₹${(v / 100000).toFixed(1)} L`;
    return `₹${v.toLocaleString('en-IN')}`;
  }

  /** "5.1 years" / "7 months" - a layperson does not read 0.42 years. */
  function span(years: number): string {
    if (years < 1) {
      const m = Math.max(1, Math.round(years * 12));
      return `${m} month${m === 1 ? '' : 's'}`;
    }
    return `${years.toFixed(1)} years`;
  }

  let risk: any = null;
  let chosen = 3;

  function profile() {
    return risk?.profiles?.find((p: any) => p.durationYears === chosen)
      ?? risk?.profiles?.[risk.profiles.length - 1];
  }

  function paintDurations() {
    const box = $('sipSeqDurations')!;
    box.innerHTML = '';
    for (const d of risk.durations) {
      const b = document.createElement('button');
      b.type = 'button';
      b.textContent = d === 1 ? '1 year' : `${d} years`;
      b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(d === chosen));
      b.className =
        'px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium transition-colors cursor-pointer border-r last:border-r-0 border-navy-200 dark:border-navy-700 ' +
        (d === chosen
          ? 'bg-gold-500/15 text-navy-900 dark:text-navy-100'
          : 'text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400 hover:bg-navy-50 dark:hover:bg-navy-900/50');
      b.addEventListener('click', () => { chosen = d; paint(); });
      box.appendChild(b);
    }
  }

  function paintBars() {
    const p = profile();
    const box = $('sipSeqBars')!;
    box.innerHTML = '';
    const worst = Math.max(...p.points.map((q: any) => q.setbackYears), 0.001);

    for (const q of p.points) {
      const row = document.createElement('div');
      row.className = 'flex items-center gap-2';

      const yr = document.createElement('span');
      yr.className = 'w-16 shrink-0 text-[11px] tabular-nums text-navy-500 dark:text-navy-400';
      yr.textContent = `year ${q.startYear}`;

      const track = document.createElement('div');
      track.className = 'flex-1 h-3.5 rounded-sm bg-navy-100 dark:bg-navy-900/60 overflow-hidden';
      const fill = document.createElement('div');
      // Colour asserts exactly one thing: this is the most damaging placement.
      fill.className = 'h-full rounded-sm ' +
        (q.startYear === p.worst.startYear
          ? 'bg-rose-500 dark:bg-rose-500'
          : 'bg-navy-400/70 dark:bg-navy-600');
      fill.style.width = `${Math.max(1.5, (q.setbackYears / worst) * 100)}%`;
      track.appendChild(fill);

      const val = document.createElement('span');
      val.className = 'w-20 shrink-0 text-right text-[11px] tabular-nums text-navy-600 dark:text-navy-400';
      val.textContent = span(q.setbackYears);

      row.append(yr, track, val);
      box.appendChild(row);
    }
  }

  function paint() {
    if (!risk?.profiles?.length) return;
    const p = profile();
    const first = p.points[0];
    const last = p.points[p.points.length - 1];

    paintDurations();
    paintBars();

    // dd-009: the average return of this profile differs from the growth
    // assumption on the page, and it is explained where it appears rather than
    // left as a discrepancy for the reader to notice and worry about.
    $('sipSeqAvg')!.textContent =
      `Every bar below assumes the same ${chosen === 1 ? 'single bad year' : `${chosen} bad years`} ` +
      `and the same ${risk.normalReturn}% in every other year — an average of ` +
      `${p.averageReturn.toFixed(1)}% a year, whichever bar you look at. Only the ` +
      `timing changes.`;

    $('sipSeqBest')!.textContent = span(first.setbackYears);
    $('sipSeqWorst')!.textContent = span(last.setbackYears);
    $('sipSeqBestEnd')!.textContent =
      `you finish with ${money(first.endReal)} instead of ${money(risk.smoothEndReal)}`;
    $('sipSeqWorstEnd')!.textContent =
      `you finish with ${money(last.endReal)} instead of ${money(risk.smoothEndReal)}`;

    const ratio = first.setbackYears > 0
      ? last.setbackYears / first.setbackYears : 0;
    $('sipSeqBoundary')!.textContent =
      `The same fall costs you ${span(first.setbackYears)} of saving at the start ` +
      `of this plan and ${span(last.setbackYears)} at the end` +
      (ratio >= 2 ? ` — ${ratio.toFixed(0)} times as much` : '') +
      `. Nothing about the fall changed. Only the year it landed in.`;
  }

  window.addEventListener('sip:sequence', (e) => {
    const d = (e as CustomEvent).detail;
    risk = d.sequence;
    if (!risk?.profiles?.length) return;
    if (!risk.durations.includes(chosen)) chosen = risk.durations[risk.durations.length - 1];
    paint();
  });
</script>
